WEBVTT

00:00:00.423 --> 00:00:04.260
Telemetry becomes much more useful when signals share consistent context.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.500
A metric that says latency increased is helpful.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:11.055
A metric that also includes the service, environment, cluster, namespace, workload,

00:00:11.055 --> 00:00:15.780
model version, deployment version, tenant class, and route is far more actionable.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.880
Labels and identifiers turn isolated signals into an investigation path.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:24.480
Important context may include service name, environment, cluster, region, namespace, node,

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.810
workload, deployment version, model name, model version, request class, data source,

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:31.850
tenant, request ID, trace ID, and correlation ID.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:33.760
Not every signal needs every label.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.640
The design decision is to include the labels that help teams answer likely questions

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.331
without creating uncontrolled cost,

00:00:39.331 --> 00:00:41.040
privacy, or cardinality problems.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.835
Correlation helps teams connect a slow user request to a specific service,

00:00:44.835 --> 00:00:48.980
model deployment, GPU node, retrieval index, or downstream dependency.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:52.775
When a request ID appears in logs, traces, events, and selected metrics,

00:00:52.775 --> 00:00:55.215
teams can move from symptom to cause with less guesswork.

00:00:55.215 --> 00:00:58.760
This is especially important in hybrid systems where ownership may cross teams and

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.180
infrastructure boundaries.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.480
Too little metadata makes investigation slow.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.275
Too much ungoverned metadata creates noise, cost, and privacy risk.

00:01:06.275 --> 00:01:08.145
Naming conventions, label discipline,

00:01:08.145 --> 00:01:12.200
and correlation rules should be treated as architecture decisions, not afterthoughts.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.360
Good context makes telemetry explainable.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:17.900
It gives responders enough shared language to ask better questions during routine

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:19.540
operations and during incidents.
